                <pb facs="0159" n="69" sameAs="frus:frus1920v02/pg69"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">837.73/24: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Cuba</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Long</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the
                            Acting Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Habana</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1920-12-14T13:00:00-05:29"><hi rendition="#italic">December 14,
                                1920—1 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received 8:46 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>347. Department’s telegram no. 254. I am told by Dr. Patterson that he has been
                    informed by the Secretary of the Interior that without the Habana-Florida cable
                    the Western Union Company could not connect its cable between Habana and
                    Barbados according to the terms of the permits it possesses at present. It was
                    the opinion of the Secretary of the Interior, however, that it would not be
                    possible to prevent the Western Union from relaying communications through its
                    office in Habana.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Long</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
